"Plots transformed residual magnitude against fitted values to reveal changes in residual spread.\n\nUse when: A regression’s constant-variance assumption needs inspection. Residual-versus-fitted points make spread changes difficult to distinguish from sign.\n\nAvoid when: The selected residual standardization is not appropriate for the model.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes fitted response values. Vertical position encodes square root of absolute standardized residuals, emphasizing spread rather than sign.\n\nBuild: Compute fitted values and appropriate standardized residuals. Transform residual magnitude with the stated square-root absolute-value rule. Plot transformed magnitudes with an optional smoother and label unusual points sparingly.\n\nCheck: Check the vertical coordinate is nonnegative and correctly transformed. Verify the residual standardization matches the fitted model. Inspect leverage and original residuals before attributing spread changes to variance alone.\n\nPitfalls: The transformed axis no longer uses original response units. A flat smoother does not prove constant variance, and isolated high points still need individual investigation.\n\nExample: Assess a synthetic delivery-cost model for increasing residual spread at larger predicted costs, using a clearly labeled scale-location axis.\n\nAlternatives: Residual plot, Leverage plot, Quantile-quantile plot."
